1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:05,487
Here is a quiz. Ten five-second clips are real. Ten

2
00:00:05,487 --> 00:00:09,372
were made by AI. Your job is to say which is which.

3
00:00:09,642 --> 00:00:13,898
Runway, a company that builds AI video models, ran

4
00:00:13,898 --> 00:00:18,556
this quiz with 1,043 people. Each one watched twenty clips.

5
00:00:18,876 --> 00:00:22,291
The average score was 57.1 percent.

6
00:00:22,511 --> 00:00:26,008
A coin flip scores 50. So the typical viewer

7
00:00:26,008 --> 00:00:28,427
was only slightly better than guessing.

8
00:00:28,697 --> 00:00:32,046
Only 99 people, about one in ten, did well

9
00:00:32,046 --> 00:00:35,654
enough that luck is an unlikely explanation.

10
00:00:35,924 --> 00:00:40,922
Real clips were judged correctly 58 percent of the time, and fake

11
00:00:40,922 --> 00:00:45,376
clips 56 percent. Neither side was much better than the other.

12
00:00:45,646 --> 00:00:51,897
Animal and building clips fell below chance, at 45 to 47 percent. Clips

13
00:00:51,897 --> 00:00:57,440
showing people, hands, or actions were easier, at 58 to 65 percent.

14
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:01,363
That is a small group. For most viewers, the honest

15
00:01:01,363 --> 00:01:04,282
answer to whether a clip is real is a guess.

16
00:01:04,702 --> 00:01:08,343
Keep in mind who ran the study. A company that sells

17
00:01:08,343 --> 00:01:11,527
these models measured how well people spot them.

18
00:01:11,847 --> 00:01:16,530
Still, the result is hard to ignore. So why can't we tell anymore?

19
00:01:16,950 --> 00:01:19,983
Here is the short answer. Every checklist

20
00:01:19,983 --> 00:01:22,852
for spotting AI video is already out of date,

21
00:01:22,852 --> 00:01:26,461
and the reason is built into how these videos are made.

22
00:01:27,281 --> 00:01:31,378
Most guides to spotting AI video are lists of flaws.

23
00:01:31,378 --> 00:01:35,797
Extra fingers. Garbled signs. Faces that blink wrong.

24
00:01:36,067 --> 00:01:39,750
Those lists are snapshots. To see why they expire,

25
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:42,601
you first need to see how a video is made.

26
00:01:42,871 --> 00:01:47,155
It starts with pure noise, like the static on an old television.

27
00:01:47,425 --> 00:01:50,457
A diffusion model learns to remove that noise

28
00:01:50,457 --> 00:01:53,670
a little at a time, guided by the text prompt.

29
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:57,528
The training works in reverse. Take a real image,

30
00:01:57,528 --> 00:02:00,574
add noise until nothing is left, and teach

31
00:02:00,574 --> 00:02:04,154
the model to undo the damage one step at a time.

32
00:02:04,424 --> 00:02:07,468
A 2020 paper from UC Berkeley described

33
00:02:07,468 --> 00:02:11,604
this approach for images, and it became influential.

34
00:02:11,874 --> 00:02:14,432
Each step nudges the picture from static

35
00:02:14,432 --> 00:02:17,169
toward something that matches the prompt.

36
00:02:17,439 --> 00:02:22,079
A video model does the same for many frames at once. The frames

37
00:02:22,079 --> 00:02:25,427
have to agree with each other, or the motion looks wrong.

38
00:02:25,847 --> 00:02:29,898
So the model is not pasting together a fake. It is learning

39
00:02:29,898 --> 00:02:33,626
what real footage looks like, and getting better at matching it.

40
00:02:33,896 --> 00:02:36,903
Notice what that means. The model learns

41
00:02:36,903 --> 00:02:40,430
statistical patterns from its training data, and patterns

42
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:43,286
are exactly what a checklist cannot capture.

43
00:02:43,556 --> 00:02:46,511
That is also why the same prompt gives different

44
00:02:46,511 --> 00:02:49,855
results each time. Each run starts from different

45
00:02:49,855 --> 00:02:53,029
static, and the model settles on a different picture.

46
00:02:53,849 --> 00:02:57,647
Take hands. Early image generators drew hands

47
00:02:57,647 --> 00:03:01,058
badly, with too many fingers or fused knuckles.

48
00:03:01,328 --> 00:03:05,729
One reason was the training data. Hands are small in most

49
00:03:05,729 --> 00:03:09,683
photos, and often hidden behind objects or other hands.

50
00:03:09,953 --> 00:03:13,649
A model can only learn from what it sees. As the data

51
00:03:13,649 --> 00:03:17,074
grew and the models got bigger, the hands got better.

52
00:03:17,344 --> 00:03:21,609
Text followed the same path. Early generators produced letters

53
00:03:21,609 --> 00:03:25,323
that looked right from a distance and fell apart up close.

54
00:03:25,593 --> 00:03:28,303
Fingers are a good example, because a hand

55
00:03:28,303 --> 00:03:32,025
has a rigid structure that people notice instantly.

56
00:03:32,025 --> 00:03:35,340
A model has to learn that structure from pixels alone.

57
00:03:35,610 --> 00:03:39,497
Blinking and reflections did too. Each flaw was a gap

58
00:03:39,497 --> 00:03:42,662
in what the model had learned, and the gaps closed.

59
00:03:43,082 --> 00:03:47,388
That is why a checklist of tells goes stale. The tells were never

60
00:03:47,388 --> 00:03:51,760
a law of physics. They were gaps in training, and gaps get filled.

61
00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:56,451
So a list written this year is a guess about next year's models.

62
00:03:56,721 --> 00:04:00,065
Each new round of training removes some old tells

63
00:04:00,065 --> 00:04:03,931
and brings new ones. The lesson is not that the flaws

64
00:04:03,931 --> 00:04:07,502
are gone. It is that the list is always behind.

65
00:04:08,322 --> 00:04:13,468
Now the second study. Researchers at UC Berkeley showed 376

66
00:04:13,468 --> 00:04:19,192
people clips from three AI video models, along with real footage.

67
00:04:19,462 --> 00:04:22,089
They changed how long each clip was shown,

68
00:04:22,089 --> 00:04:24,713
from a single frame up to eight seconds.

69
00:04:24,983 --> 00:04:28,029
For the fake clips, watching longer helped.

70
00:04:28,029 --> 00:04:31,496
Accuracy rose from about 58 percent on one

71
00:04:31,496 --> 00:04:35,142
frame to about 75 percent at eight seconds.

72
00:04:35,412 --> 00:04:37,397
Seedance was the easiest model

73
00:04:37,397 --> 00:04:40,330
for viewers to catch, and Sora the hardest.

74
00:04:40,750 --> 00:04:44,133
For the real clips, it did not. Accuracy stayed

75
00:04:44,133 --> 00:04:47,347
near 60 percent, however long people watched.

76
00:04:47,767 --> 00:04:49,478
Think about what that means.

77
00:04:49,798 --> 00:04:52,820
Looking longer helped people catch the fakes.

78
00:04:52,820 --> 00:04:55,168
It did not help them with the real clips.

79
00:04:55,438 --> 00:04:58,450
Viewers in that study leaned on motion, and looked

80
00:04:58,450 --> 00:05:01,169
for movement that broke what they expected.

81
00:05:01,439 --> 00:05:05,693
For real clips, a longer look earned no more trust than a glance.

82
00:05:06,113 --> 00:05:09,884
So the cost of fake video is not only being fooled.

83
00:05:09,884 --> 00:05:13,652
The bigger risk is that real video stops being believed.

84
00:05:13,922 --> 00:05:16,112
A checklist of tells will never show

85
00:05:16,112 --> 00:05:19,101
that cost. Only a habit of checking will.

86
00:05:19,921 --> 00:05:24,229
If eyes and checklists keep failing, what still works? Three things.

87
00:05:24,499 --> 00:05:29,584
First, provenance. Some cameras and editing tools attach a signed

88
00:05:29,584 --> 00:05:34,746
record called Content Credentials, under a standard called C2PA.

89
00:05:34,746 --> 00:05:38,768
It says who captured or generated a file, and with which tool.

90
00:05:39,038 --> 00:05:42,506
The weakness is that many platforms strip this record

91
00:05:42,506 --> 00:05:46,671
when a file is uploaded, and a screenshot removes it too.

92
00:05:46,671 --> 00:05:49,832
So a missing record proves nothing in either direction.

93
00:05:50,102 --> 00:05:53,814
Second, invisible watermarks. Google's SynthID

94
00:05:53,814 --> 00:05:57,394
hides a signal inside images, video, and audio.

95
00:05:57,664 --> 00:06:02,714
On October 7, 2026, Google opened its SynthID Detector

96
00:06:02,714 --> 00:06:07,212
to anyone, in English. It also reads watermarks

97
00:06:07,212 --> 00:06:11,137
from partners such as OpenAI, NVIDIA, and Kakao.

98
00:06:11,407 --> 00:06:15,531
Google's tool is free to use, and it reads only the watermarks

99
00:06:15,531 --> 00:06:18,983
it knows. Anything without one, or with a mark

100
00:06:18,983 --> 00:06:22,909
from a source it does not check, comes back with no answer.

101
00:06:23,329 --> 00:06:28,112
A match tells you a watermark is there. No match tells you nothing,

102
00:06:28,112 --> 00:06:31,758
because many AI clips carry no SynthID mark at all.

103
00:06:32,078 --> 00:06:35,699
Those two tools are useful, and both have holes.

104
00:06:35,699 --> 00:06:38,086
That is why they are not the whole answer.

105
00:06:38,356 --> 00:06:41,945
Neither one answers whether a specific clip is real.

106
00:06:41,945 --> 00:06:43,736
They answer narrower questions,

107
00:06:43,736 --> 00:06:46,771
and you have to know which question you are asking.

108
00:06:47,591 --> 00:06:50,000
Third, the method that does not depend

109
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:53,180
on any tool. Ask who posted the clip first.

110
00:06:53,450 --> 00:06:57,284
A repost is not a source. Find the earliest upload

111
00:06:57,284 --> 00:07:00,651
you can, and see what the account posted before it.

112
00:07:00,921 --> 00:07:04,791
Be wary of a clip that arrives with a dramatic caption

113
00:07:04,791 --> 00:07:08,972
and no origin. Real footage often arrives that way too,

114
00:07:08,972 --> 00:07:12,486
which is why the origin matters more than the look.

115
00:07:12,756 --> 00:07:15,011
Then look for a second, independent

116
00:07:15,011 --> 00:07:17,383
source that reports the same event.

117
00:07:17,653 --> 00:07:19,787
Check whether the place, the time,

118
00:07:19,787 --> 00:07:22,894
and the details line up with what else is known.

119
00:07:23,164 --> 00:07:26,466
A reverse image search and a look at the original

120
00:07:26,466 --> 00:07:29,222
post can settle many cases in a minute.

121
00:07:29,542 --> 00:07:34,196
None of these asks you to find a flaw in the pixels. That is the point.

122
00:07:34,466 --> 00:07:37,599
So the question is no longer whether you can tell

123
00:07:37,599 --> 00:07:40,887
AI video from real video. Most people cannot

124
00:07:40,887 --> 00:07:44,311
do it reliably, and that is not a personal failing.

125
00:07:44,581 --> 00:07:47,831
None of these checks needs a new tool. They work

126
00:07:47,831 --> 00:07:51,006
the same way next year, whatever the models look like.

127
00:07:51,426 --> 00:07:55,816
The question is what you will still trust when a real video shows up.

128
00:07:56,086 --> 00:07:58,805
Tell us in the comments. The last time

129
00:07:58,805 --> 00:08:02,320
you were not sure a video was AI, how did you decide?

